==Latvian==
{{slim-wikipedia|lang=lv}}
[[File:1080PoisonWarning gobeirne.png|thumb|250px|Inde (etiķete)]]

===Etymology===
A 20th-century [[neologism]], introduced in the Scientific Terminology Dictionary (Riga, 1922) to replace a previous [[Germanism]], {{m|lv|ģifts}}. The word was coined by shortening the (old-fashioned, dialectal) word {{m|lv|indeve||illness, disease; bad disposition; evil spirit; poison}}, which [[w:Jānis Endzelīns|J. Endzelīns]] considered either an old [[w:en:Curonian language|Curonian]] term or a [[borrowing]] from {{etyl|lt|-}} ([[cf]]. {{etyl|lt|-}} dialectal {{m|lt|indėvė||poison; evil, evil spirit}}), perhaps formed from a prefix {{m|lv|*in-}} ({{cog|lv|ie-}}) and the verb {{m|lv|dot||to give}} or {{m|lv|dēt||to lay (eggs); orig. to put}}. The meaning evolution would be similar to that of {{cog|de|Gift}}: from “something given, put (in)” to “poison.” Another possiblity, suggested by the “evil spirit” meaning of the {{etyl|lt|-}} cognate (also attested in older {{etyl|lv|-}} sources as a name for the devil), is that {{m|lv|indeve}} might come from {{m|lv|*in-||negative}} + {{m|lv|dievs}}, i.e. “no-god” > “evil, evil spirit” ([[cf]]. similarly formed {{m|lv|nedievs}}). It is also possible that two similar words, meaning “disease” and “evil spirit,” became homophonous and merged as {{m|lv|indeve}}. It has also been suggested that {{cog|dum|inde||end; death}}, {{m|dum|inden||to end life, to die}} could also have influenced {{m|lv|indeve}}, given the strong presence of Dutch sailors and craftsmen in the times of the old [[w:en:Duchy of Courland and Semigallia|Duchy of Courland]] (1561-1726).<ref>{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

